<h2>
Different Types of QR Codes and Their Application Areas
</h2>


<p>
There are mainly two types of QR codes: Static QR codes and Dynamic QR codes. Both help users connect physical and digital experiences efficiently.
</p>




<h3>
Static QR Codes
</h3>


<p>
Static QR codes store fixed information permanently. Once created, the information inside the QR code cannot be changed. They are useful for permanent data such as website links, contact details, messages, and business information.
</p>


<p>
For example, restaurants can use static QR codes to provide access to fixed online menus. Companies can also place them on business cards, product packaging, and promotional materials.
</p>





<h3>
Dynamic QR Codes
</h3>


<p>
Dynamic QR codes provide flexibility because the destination information can be updated after creation. They are useful for businesses that frequently change campaigns, promotions, or content.
</p>


<p>
For example, restaurants can use the same QR code and update the connected menu whenever new products or price changes are introduced.
</p>






<h2>
QR Code Creation Best Practices
</h2>



<ul>

<li>
<b>Use Relevant Information:</b> Create QR codes that direct users to valuable content such as websites, landing pages, or useful resources.
</li>


<li>
<b>Use Appropriate Size:</b> Ensure the QR code is large enough and positioned correctly for easy scanning.
</li>


<li>
<b>Optimize Destination Page:</b> Make sure the connected webpage works properly on mobile devices.
</li>


<li>
<b>Provide Context:</b> Add instructions like "Scan to visit our website" to encourage engagement.
</li>


<li>
<b>Test Before Publishing:</b> Scan the QR code from different devices before using it publicly.
</li>


</ul>






